A||||3||Raydar||Text of American Apology to the Chinese. ;)||||6||
Z||000000||Raydar||04-11-2001||05:45 PM||||Dear China,<P> We're sorry that you don't train your fighter pilots better. As a token of our apology, here's a copy of Microsoft Flight Simulator 2000.<P> We're sorry that you're front-line fighter planes can't outmaneuver a 35 year old prop-driven airliner. Perhaps you'd like to consider purchasing some surplus 1950's era Lockheed Starfighters from Taiwan. (Who just replaced all theirs with shiny new F-16's)<P> We're sorry that you believe your territorial waters extend all the way to Australia. For future reference, here's an American 6th grade geography textbook.  (Please take note of the Copyright information printed inside the cover.)<P> We're sorry that you can't seem to see your part of this incident.  We know that it may seem easier to blame others than to take responsibility. Consider this while we build several new Aegis destroyers for our friends in the Republic of China (Taiwan).<P> We're especially sorry for treating you with such respect for the last 20 years.  We'll definitely rethink this policy, and will probably go back to treating you like a common untrustworthy street gang very soon.<P> We're very sorry for ever granting you Most-Favored-Nation trading status. This will be rectified at the soonest possible opportunity.<P>    Sincerely,<P>    The United States of America<BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||6
Z||000001||gixxer||04-11-2001||06:23 PM||||Yes, you have to wonder about the quality of a fighter pilot who lets himself get blindsided by a converted Lockheed Electra.<BR>Talk about "loss of face"!<BR>Makes me want to hop into a Cessna 152  "Acemaker" and make a living as a soldier of fortune, knocking down Chinese airforce jets with a BB gun. <BR>Or a slingshot.<BR>Or an old donut.<BR>Phfft.<BR>Plink!<BR>Splash.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||RiceCooker||04-11-2001||06:24 PM||||Here's the one I read earlier today<P>--------------------<BR>Glorious Leaders of China<BR>34 All Hail the Workers Ave. <BR>Beijing, China <BR>Honorable Sirs:<P>We are really, really sorry that our Navy's sluggish, propeller-driven <BR>reconnaissance aircraft was traveling too slowly to get out of the way of <BR>your martyred pilot's double-supersonic, state-of-the-art F-8 fighter jet. <P>And we are even sorrier that you, the gentle leaders of the People's <BR>Republic, had to suffer the shock -- the shock! -- of learning that we've <BR>been cravenly keyhole-peeping through the Bamboo Curtain. <P>Even though our aircraft have been flying these same missions for more than <BR>45 years -- since the Eisenhower administration -- we are truly sorry that <BR>they suddenly have come to your attention in such an unfortunate manner. <P>We are deeply sorry, as well, that our crippled aircraft so belligerently <BR>landed on your territory without first seeking permission by filing the <BR>proper paperwork. That would have allowed your famously speedy and efficient <BR>bureaucracy to leap, tiger-like, to the task of providing said clearance <BR>within the perfectly respectable waiting period of, say, eight months or so. <P>We sincerely hope that our terrible lapse of judgment in this incident does <BR>not jeopardize the ``exchange programs'' that allow your nuclear scientists <BR>to wander the hallways of our research facilities, stuffing their backpacks <BR>with pencils, pens, computer disks, hard drives and other harmless <BR>bric-a-brac. <P>On a personal note, we'd like to apologize, as well -- humbly, sincerely and <BR>on bended knee -- for stupidly buying all those tennis shoes, polyester <BR>calypso shirts and Christmas bulbs from you. <P>Shame on us. In forcing the manufacture of these trifles, we are taking up <BR>valuable time that your prison inmates and elementary-school students could <BR>be putting to much better use -- say, getting busy with those hundreds of <BR>thousands of black berets that are on back-order for our own military forces. <P>Let us hope, in our sorrow, that this unfortunate incident does not disrupt <BR>our normal trade relations, under which we give you a boatload of satellite <BR>telemetry technology in exchange for a boatload of firecrackers, plastic <BR>chopsticks and semi-laundered political donations. <P>In closing, we sincerely hope that this diplomatic dust-up does not <BR>jeopardize Beijing's chances for hosting the Summer Olympics. We have been so <BR>looking forward to a healthy, face-to-face competition in the 400 Meter <BR>Mendacity Relay and the Honorable All-Steroid Hong-Kong-to-Haiphong <BR>Trans-Pacific Women's Swim-Team Marathon. (You go, gals!) <P>We're sorrier than you could ever imagine. <P>Signed, <P>Heywood Jablome<P>||||reg||6
Z||000003||Raydar||04-11-2001||07:37 PM||||Ricecooker,<P>Touche'! Even better!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||14
